ature Explorer Passport Sports and leisure About the City of Merri-bek In my area Love Merri-bek, Buy Local Building and business Planning and building Planning Building Strategic planning Planning, building and environmental health fines Business Why Merri-bek for business? Starting or taking over a business Business permits Business support and programs Find a local business Contact Economic Development for business support Coworking and maker spaces Make your business accessible Projects Conversations Merri-bek Building and park projects Current projects Completed projects Transport projects Environment projects Energy projects My Council About Council Councillors CEO and executive team Renaming Merri-bek Sister cities and friendship cities Our sustainability story Council budget Advisory committees Council Plan and Community Vision Governance rules Council and committee meetings Council meetings Planning and Related Matters meetings Advisory Committees External committees Livestreaming Engage Conversations Merri-bek Petitions Committees Social media user guidelines Careers at Merri-bek Current vacancies Applying for a job Working at Merri-bek Work experience First Peoples Careers News and publications News Annual Reports Aged and community support Newsletter Merri-bek Community News My Merri-bek e-newsletter Our Candid Community Podcast Strategies, policies and collected data Policies and strategies Collected data Community infrastructure planning Online privacy policy Public Interest Disclosures Tenders Grants and awards Arts grants Community Grants Program Merri-bek Community Awards Local laws and legislation Merri-bek local laws Local law and animal fines Local law permits Documents for public review Freedom of information Contact Contact us Report an issue Give our staff a compliment Complaints Information in your language Emergency management Storms and flooding preparedness Municipal Emergency Management Plan Emergency numbers Preparing for Emergencies: Before, During and After Extreme heat and heat health Council advocacy Advocacy Priorities Submissions on key issues Advocacy Partnerships Council service finder Our sites: Active Merri-bek Conversations Merri-bek Love Merri-bek, Buy Local Zero Carbon Merri-bek Alerts Merri-bek City Council is committed to being a Child Safe organisation and has zero tolerance for child abuse. Clear alerts Applying for a job Home My Council Careers at Merri-bek Applying for a job On this page: How to apply All available positions are advertised on our Current Vacancies page. The job advertisement will provide you with information on the requirements of applying for the role. Please fill out an application using our online recruitment system. You will be required to upload your resume, and a cover letter outlining your relevant skills and experience. You do not need to respond to the individual Key Selection Criteria outside of our standard application questions. Please also upload any other relevant documents such as your COVID-19 Vaccination Certificate. Cover letter Your cover letter should summarise your key skills and experience in no more than one page and provide a valuable snapshot of what you have to offer. Your letter should be structured in a logical way. Date Contact details Include your name, and contact number and e-mail address as a minimum Job details Include the employer's name, job title and position number. Introduction Write about why you are interested in the role. In a sentence or two, explain why you are the best candidate for the job - you need to grab your audience at the beginning of your cover letter. Explaining why you are the best person for the advertising job. Body Demonstrate how you meet the experience and skill requirements of the position. Identify your strengths and accomplishments and highlight some key points from your resume and provide supporting evidence. Conclusion Thank the reader for their time and consideration and indicate your availability for an interview. Pay attention to writing style, spelling, grammar and punctuation. A clear writing style will ensure your letter is easily understood by the reader. It is important to be concise while still giving enough detail. To do this, cut out unnecessary words, avoid jargon and avoid overly complex sentences. Be positive in content, tone and word choice. Resume A resume or curriculum vitae provides a summary of your skills, employment history, experience, knowledge and abilities relevant to the position you are applying for. In a resume it is important to demonstrate your achievements, what you have learned and the commitment level involved. By describing your achievements, we will get a clearer picture of what you did to develop the skills and whether you have the qualities and experience we are looking for. Your resume should include your: Name contact details - phone number(s), email as a minimum Employment history – start with your most recent job and work backwards, include all relevant work history, including volunteering and work experience Education and qualifications - a brief summary of your education and qualifications, including the name of the institution where you studied, the course title and the date completed. Also include any short training courses you have completed. Ensure you are able to produce documentary evidence of any formal qualifications required for the job. Demonstrated skills - this may include information about your ability to use relevant software programs - indicate if your skills are basic, intermediate or advanced. Special achievements - highlight any special achievements, such as community work, volunteering, prizes or awards Referees – contact details of at least two referees who can support your claims in relation to the position. Your referees could include a previous employer, manager or supervisor; a teacher or trainer; and a coordinator of voluntary work. Make sure your referees know they may be contacted and have details about the job you are applying for. Tips: Use dot points to separate the individual skills you have gained through previous positions and past experience. Use headings and sub-headings to clearly separate different areas such as employment history (including month and year of employment), skills, knowledge and abilities. Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO) Merri-bek City Council is an Equal Employment Opportunity employer. We are committed to ensuring that all employees are treated fairly regardless of their background or personal characteristics. Visit Victorian Human Rights Commission for more information about discrimination and workplace rights. Quicklinks I want to: View current job vacancies See tips on applying for a job Explore this section Explore this section My Council About Council Councillors Mayor and Councillors Councillor conduct Council elections CEO and executive team Renaming Merri-bek Sister cities and friendship cities Our sustainability story Council budget Advisory committees Council Plan and Community Vision Governance rules Council and committee meetings Council meetings Council meeting dates, locations and questions Next Council meeting agenda Council meeting minutes and past agendas Planning and Related Matters meetings Planning and Related Matters meetings, dates and locations Next Planning and Related Matters meeting agenda Planning and Related Matters meeting minutes and past agendas Advisory Committees Audit and Risk Committee Children's Reference Group Environmental Sustainability and Climate Resilience Advisory Committee Friends of Aileu Community Committee Gender Equality Reference Group Health, Wellbeing and Social Inclusion Advisory Committee LGBTIQA+ Reference Group Liveability Advisory Committee Merri-bek Arts Advisory Committee Older Persons Reference Group Past Advisory Committees and Reference Groups Strategic Transport Reference Group External committees Livestreaming Engage Conversations Merri-bek Petitions Committee